 "about": "Facts for SYNTHETIC home-care referrals. A 'states' fact states the claims it lists; a 'distractor' mentions the topic of the claims it lists without stating them, and is used only when those claims are false and its 'when' claims are true; a 'filler' gives the reason for the referral. 'must' holds the evidence: every group needs one of its patterns in the written referral. 'topics' are the patterns a referral may not contain for a false claim that has no distractor, so the writer cannot add a need the answer key lacks. Patterns are case-insensitive regular expressions. Placeholders: {name} (title and surname), {She}/{she} (subject pronoun), {her} (possessive). Pool A is for the dev set, pool B for the held-out set, pool C for the fresh set, pool D for the second fresh set. Pool C's visit plans are named the ways councils name home-care calls (meal-named call bands, clock times, counts), and its insulin facts follow the government's description of delegated healthcare activities (a nurse hands the task to trained care workers). Pool D's visit plans cover every kind the earlier sets showed, in new words: lists of call names, care-plan shorthand counts (OD, BD, TDS, QDS: once, twice, three and four times a day), counts with the day's ends or alone, clock times, and first and last thing; its insulin distractors leave the task with a nurse, a partner or the person.",
